ed prior to  the freezing  of funds it censored us for having  presented our plan without prior  arrangements as regrettable in view of  the delicate domestic situation  and directed us to submit without fail  plan b  at our next meeting stating so long as  america's acceptance of the above cannot  be had  it cannot be helped should the  negotiation  be ruptured on the 20th of november  plan b was submitted the primary reasons  why america refused to give her consent  to plan b  were as follows discontinuation of aid  to chiang kai-shek  is tantamount to discontinuation of aid  to great britain  america cannot accept it the transfer of  troops in southern french indochina  mainly to the north would still keep the  troops of other nations  in the southwest pacific area the  statements of important government  leaders as usual  advocate the armed aggrandization policy  there is none such as would give  credence to this peaceful intention of  japan  and finally the fact that japan's  furious request for a reply without  consideration of the fact that each of  the nations concerned must request their  respective government for instructions  gave the impression as though it were a  demand  secretary of state hull's attitude which  had been friendly and cheerful until the  conference of the 26th of november  appeared to have changed suddenly i came  to think of it later  but it was because of an important  reason dispatch number 726 from togo to  nomura  was sent on the day of my departure the  5th of november  this was one of the reasons for the  misunderstanding that i had come to  america with the knowledge that a final  ultimatum  would be submitted that this message had  been intercepted by america has been  explained at the beginning of this book  furthermore  this message had been completely  mistranslated  and it appears as if the translation was  completed and shown to the secretary of  state  on about the 22nd the message in  question was the instruction from to