kyo  concerning  plan a which is one of our final plans  although the original message had as its  beginning the phrase  as a final conciliatory plan america's  intercepted message  read as a final revised ultimatum  it is only logical that america  including secretary of state hull  became offended upon reading this  mistranslation and reached the  conclusion  that japan possessed no sincerity  our meeting with the president was held  on the 27th of november  amidst such an oppressive atmosphere the  previous meeting that is the meeting of  the 17th was held in the official  reception room of the white house  but this was held in a room appearing to  be the office of the president  of course the secretary of state was  present the situation then was not only  pressing for me  according to the literature made public  since the end of the war the president  too appeared to have been fully  cognizant of the seriousness  of the situation in spite of this  the attitude of the president towards us  was suave and graceful  and cheerful after we had taken our  seats and ambassador nomura had accepted  a cigarette offered by the president  the president personally lit a match and  attempted to light the cigarette  but ambassador nomura having only one  good eye  could not bring the point of his  cigarette to the light extended by the  president  the president laughed and extended his  hand further to light the cigarette  consequently the meeting was begun  ostensibly in an extremely amicable  atmosphere  the president then pointed out that  japan and america had suffered together  as allies during the last war because  germany was not able to understand the  psychology  of the other nations continuing further  he explained that he was happy to hear  that there have been some peace  advocates in japan  and that he still had great hopes of  japan and america arriving at a peaceful  settlement  in all probability the president must  have been receiving in rapid succession  from the american authorities stationed  along the coast of french indochina and  china  reports of the movement of our land and  sea forces the unloading of various  weapons  and the movements of our transports the  situation wherein the negotiation would  become much more difficult as the  deployment of our strength became known  was gradually becoming a reality just as  foreign minister togo  had feared the time of my departure from  tokyo  the president further explained that as  stated by envoy kurusu recently  he was fully aware that the frame of  mind of the japanese people  who had suffered four years of war could  not be measured by the standard of  peaceful america but the fact that there  had been no peaceful statements from the  leaders of japan during the discussions  between the secretary and state and us  had made this negotiation very difficult  finally the conference ended with  ambassador nomura  relying on his friendship of 30 years  with the president appealing to him  to break the deadlock with his statement  in other words with this discussion  our effort to make america reconsider  and bring about a change in the deadlock  ended in a complete failure  the negotiation was now in a practically  hopeless  situation  the secretary of state who had been  silent to the newspapers and others  concerning the negotiation in the past  voluntarily made a public statement  concerning the meeting  on the 26th responding to this the  newspapers  with such headlines as hull hands peace  plan to japan and  america rejects second munich plan  emphasized such points of  secretary of state hull's so-called four  basic principles  against which no one could raise an  objection as abstract arguments and  wrote that it was up to japan  whether to accept america's proposal the  japanese government's dispatch explained  that it regarded america's note of the  26th  as surprising and regrettable and  instructed us that japan  absolutely cannot consider this note as  an overture and that there was no  alternative  but to submit to them the imperial  government's opinion  to be wired in two or three days and in  reality  to break off the negotiation  however just before this premier tojo's  american and british expulsion speech  was greatly played up in the american  newspapers on the 30th  of november above all the part claiming  the british  and american exploitation of the asiatic  races  should be exterminated with vengeance  was given larger publicity  since the prime minister had clamored  for the expulsion of the opposing  country in which not one but two  of these ambassadors were busily engaged  in a negotiation  it was not unreasonable for the  repercussion it caused within the  various quarters of america  to have been so strong as to be beyond  the imagination  of people in japan  the first of december meeting with the  secretary of state was held at the  department of state under such an  extremely  tense atmosphere at the very outset the  secretary of state touched upon the  premier's speech  with a grave expression i explained that  i believe the premier speech probably  had been twisted and exaggerated stating  that  if only a certain portion and not the  complete text of anyone's speech is  given the real intention of the talker  could be misinterpreted into something  with an entirely different meaning  then the secretary of state revealed  that he was receiving in rapid  succession from the american authorities  on the spot reports concerning the  latest deployment of our troops  in the southwest pacific area he  explained that we would be able to fully  understand how america felt today if our  positions were reversed  and the japanese army and navy were  placed in a similar position  as the close of the meeting i stated  that the motto don't sell china down the  river  was prevalent in america but would not  japan and america be selling themselves  down the river  if they should eventually go to war  against each other because of the china  question in reply the secretary of state  nodded  but only repeated that in view of the  present situation  he wanted japan to open the way for  fresh development  through the improvement of her speeches  under secretary of state sumner wells  who substituted for secretary of state  hull  who was ill requested us to call he  handed us a document stating that it was  from the direct order  of the president it pertained to the  recent strengthening of our troops  in the southern french indochina area it  was a harsh document of warning  announcing beforehand  that appropriate actions would be taken  in terms of america's  national defence depending on japan's  reply  the document received from the under  secretary was immediately wired to our  government  but our government's reply was very  brusque  it stated that the rumor of the  strengthening of our troops was  considered to be an exaggerated report  of the strengthening of our troops in a  section of northern french indochina  initiated primarily for the purpose of  preparing against the chinese troops  recently active along the border as we  saw it when compared with the reports  believed to be in the hands of america  then  this response will hardly be  satisfactory to them  at the same time the department of state  issued a report that there were 125 000  japanese troops in french indochina in  two divisions under the escort of  warships  appeared to have finally become critical  consequently we who were unaware that  the government had held an imperial  conference  and had already decided on the war were  deeply concerned about the situation and  requested the government to give us a  reply clarifying our peaceful  intentions a little more if it desired a  settlement of the negotiation  however our government's reply was that  it was impossible to explain any further  since it involved the secret  the 7th of december was sunday i heard  from naval attache ichiro yokoyama who  had called in the morning that many  messages seemed to have been delivered  to the embassy office  then i received a report from secretary  yuki that the imperial government's  opinion  mentioned in the government's  instructions of the 28th of november  had arrived since numerous messages had  arrived simultaneously that morning  there appeared to have been considerable  confusion in the embassy office in the  wireless room  the final message specifying the time  for the handing over of the memorandum  arrived about 10 30 am of the seventh  and since  instructions to the effect that typists  should not be permitted to type the  memorandum for the preservation of its  secrecy  a higher grade secretary who was not  adept at typing  began typing it from the morning of the  seventh it did not progress as quickly  as expected  ambassador nomura too was considerably  worried  and it became evident that the important  memorandum would not be ready for 1pm  the time arranged with secretary of  state hull the appointment was  unavoidably put off  until 2pm i together with ambassador  nomura went to the state department  it was 2 20 p.m when we finally met with  the secretary of state  after looking at the clock in the room  and announcing the time to us the  secretary of state began reading the  memorandum which was handed to him  in the meantime the excitement gradually  caused his hand holding the memorandum  to begin  shaking finally after having finished  reading it  the secretary of state made the now  historically famous remark  throughout the nine months of  negotiation i never did say a lie  and in my 50 years of public service i  have never seen a document so  full of fabrication and falsehood  after we had heard this we silently left  the secretary's office  as this was sunday and the meeting had  been arranged suddenly that morning  there were not many people at the  department of state and practically no  reporters when we arrived there  when we were about to leave they began  gathering gradually but we refrained  from replying to their inquiries and  returned to the embassy  it was then that we heard the news of  the attack on  pearl harbor as soon as the automobile  we were riding entered  the front gate of the embassy was closed  and the police  started warding off the crowd which  began together  when i entered my room upon returning to  the embassy i saw finance officer  tsutomu nishiyama  standing dumbfounded as he was an expert  on finance and economics and had been  residing in and studying in america for  many years  he knew better than anyone else that  japan absolutely would be no match for  america  in protracted war with a feeling that we  lacked the one thing we needed we  blankly stared at each other  i could think of nothing to say  in the meantime military attaches saburo  soda  with tears in his eyes came to my room  and expressed his sympathies  the other members of the embassy all had  a dejected look  i certainly was deeply moved by the  varied feeling of sympathy shown by  these people  toward me who had failed in an important  mission  [Music]  you
 

